#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171873 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272baac4-5bd1-5539-9493-27e9728fa7eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Sum of Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "24cbb085-f4b4-5bdb-add2-3ba78337f37f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9080" :question/problem "Compute $\\textrm E[5X-2Y]$ if $\\textrm E[X]=1$ and $\\textrm E[Y] = 2$." :question/worked-solution "For any random variables $X,Y$ and any constants $a,b$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[aX+bY] = a \\textrm E[X] + b \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[5X - 2Y] &= 5E[X] - 2E[Y] \\\\\n&= 5(1) - 2(2) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f205e5f7-b2f0-5daa-9392-dc627a5cb277" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Product of Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f69db820-d94c-5c04-a3e2-34ad50091d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9081" :question/problem "The probability distributions of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n| $x$$-4$$-2$$2$$4$$f(x)$$\\dfrac{1}{6}$$\\dfrac{1}{3}$$\\dfrac{1}{3}$$\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |  | $y$$1$$2$$3$$f(y)$$\\dfrac{1}{4}$$\\dfrac{1}{4}$$\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nFind $\\textrm E[XY]$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y]=\\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= -4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = 0 \\\\\nE[Y] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X\\cdot Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\cdot \\textrm E[Y] = 0 \\cdot \\dfrac{9}{4} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbf1aa8a-50e6-5fff-bcde-8d621ad8daad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Expected Value of a Sum of Random Variables in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "1c91327d-01a2-5937-a26d-741205897a28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9085" :question/problem "Andrea has two dice. One of them is fair, and the other is not. The rolls of the unfair die follow the probability mass function $f(y)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected sum of the outcomes of $2$ throws of the fair die and $4$ throws of the unfair one?\n\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(y)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{16}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_i$ be the $i$th score obtained from the fair die, and let $Y_j$ be the $j$th score obtained from the unfair die. Also, let $X$ and $Y$ represent the scores obtained from a typical throw of the fair and unfair die, respectively.\n\nThen, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^2 X_i + \\sum_{j=1}^4 Y_j \\right]\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[∑_(i = 1)^(2)X_{i} + ∑_(j = 1)^(4)Y_{j}] &= ∑_(i = 1)^(2)E[X_{i}] + ∑_(j = 1)^(4)E[Y_{j}] \\\\\n&= 2E[X] + 4E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the last equality above is true because\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X_i] = \\textrm E[X]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y_j] = \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i \\leq 2,\\: 1\\leq j\\leq 4\n$$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\nE[Y] &= 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} + 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[2X+4Y]=2\\textrm E[X]+4\\textrm E[Y]= 2\\cdot \\dfrac{7}{2}+4\\cdot \\dfrac{13}{4} =20\n$$"}]}
